['```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <iterator>
#include <utility>

std::vector<int> insertAfterTargetValue(std::vector<int>& vec, int target, int insertionValue, int maxDurationMs) {
    auto start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    auto it = std::lower_bound(vec.cbegin(), vec.cmiddle(), target);
    if (it != vec.cend() && *it == target) {
        vec.insert(++it, insertionValue);
    } else if (std::distance(vec.cbegin(), it) <= vec.size() / 2) {
        vec.insert(it, insertionValue);
    } else {
        vec.push_back(target);
        vec.push_back(insertionValue);
    }

    auto end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
    if (std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(end - start).count() > maxDurationMs) {
        std::cerr << "Operation exceeded the specified duration limit." << std::endl;
    }

    return vec;
}
```

This code defines a function `insertAfterTargetValue`, which takes a vector of integers, a target value, an insertion value, and a maximum acceptable operation duration in milliseconds. The function uses the lower_bound algorithm to find the first occurrence of the target value in the vector efficiently. If the target value is found within the first half of the vector, it is inserted immediately after the target. Otherwise, the target and insertion values are appended to the end of the vector.

The function ensures that the operation duration does not exceed the specified limit by checking the elapsed time using `std::chrono`. If the operation takes too long, it outputs an error message to the standard error stream (cerr). The code also optimizes for space efficiency by inserting the new element directly into the vector without creating temporary containers.']

