    LogBuffer* buffer = get_log_buffer();
    log_buffer_append(buffer, message, length);

    if (flush_immediately) {
        log_buffer_flush(buffer);
    } else if (time(NULL) % FLUSH_INTERVAL == 0) {
        log_buffer_flush(buffer);
    }
}
```

This code provides a simple implementation of the task using dynamic memory management. The `LogBuffer` structure stores the log data, length, and a lock for synchronization. A function called `log()` is responsible for appending messages to the buffer, flushing it automatically every `FLUSH_INTERVAL` seconds or on demand if `flush_immediately` is set.

Error handling for file I/O operations is implemented by checking the return value of `fopen()`. For network logging, you can create another function that handles network communication and error handling appropriately. Make sure to optimize memory usage as much as possible while ensuring maintainability and scalability.']

