In this example, I provided a simple buffering mechanism by transforming the incoming data into JSON format, which helps optimize memory usage when dealing with varying load conditions. Keep in mind that you may need to modify and expand this codebase to meet your specific project requirements or performance goals."]

[" Here's a simplified implementation of an asynchronous data aggregator in JavaScript using Promises and a buffer with a specified maximum size. This example also includes a backpressure mechanism to prevent buffer overflow and ensure data integrity.

```javascript
class DataAggregator {
  constructor(bufferSize) {
    this._buffer = [];
    this._bufferSize = bufferSize;
    this._activeStreams = 0;
    this._resolvedPromises = [];
  }

  async consume() {
    if (this._resolvedPromises.length > 0) return Promise.resolve(this._resolvedPromises.shift());

    const data = await this._buffer[this._buffer.length - 1][1];
    this._buffer.pop();
    this._activeStreams--;

    return data;
  }

  async enqueue(data) {
    if (this._buffer.length >= this._bufferSize && !this._activeStreams) {
      await new Promise((resolve) => {
        this._resolvedPromises.push(resolve);
      });
    }

    this._buffer.push([data, new Promise(() => {})]);
    this._activeStreams++;
  }
}
```

Usage:

```javascript
const agg = new DataAggregator(10);

// Simulate multiple asynchronous streams
async function stream1() {
  for (let i = 0; i < 50; i++) await agg.enqueue(`stream1-${i}`);
}

async function stream2() {
  for (let i = 51; i < 65; i++) await agg.enqueue(`stream2-${i}`);
}

// Consume data from the aggregator
const consumeStream = async () => {
  while (true) {
    const data = await agg.consume();
    console.log(data); // process the data here
  }
};

// Start multiple streams and consumption simultaneously
stream1().then(() => stream2());
consumeStream();
```

