3. Logging errors with a simple `log_error()` function, considering the impact on system monitoring and debugging capabilities by writing messages only when logging is enabled.
4. Optimizing for performance by minimizing memory allocations and deallocations during the function execution using a single malloc() call and careful handling of memory within the loop.
5. Integrating with an external logging framework to log successful allocations and any errors encountered during the process.
6. Supporting a configuration parameter to control the randomness seed for CRCFLAG generation using a global variable `seed`.
