The mutex is used to ensure thread safety during concurrent access. The code allows handling varying loads by dynamically managing the number of free sessions. To enable seamless synchronization between in-memory and disk-based storage, you can integrate an external data store as needed in the comments section.

For high concurrency and heavy update patterns, consider using a thread-safe container like `std::list` or `std::vector` instead of raw pointers. Also, keep in mind that this example does not provide optimizations for memory overhead or I/O operations, which can be addressed with techniques such as caching, batching, and asynchronous processing depending on your specific use case requirements.']

